From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Subject: [PATCH] eci_pcm: runtime choice of upsampling method

Upstream picks the upsampling method once per process out of EVV_UPSAMPLE,
which is an experiment knob and not a setting. The Android front end
offers it as one -- see Audio Optimization -- so it needs a call:
evv_setUpsampleMethod records a process-wide override that cvt_method
honours every time a converter is (re)built, and re-setting the sample
rate after the call rebuilds the converter and puts the new method in
force at once. Negative (the default) keeps the old behaviour exactly.

diff --git a/src/eci/sound/eci_pcm.c b/src/eci/sound/eci_pcm.c
index 76cc88c..a66b1eb 100644
--- a/src/eci/sound/eci_pcm.c
+++ b/src/eci/sound/eci_pcm.c
@@ -491,6 +491,18 @@ typedef struct AudioConverter {
 
 const uint32_t pcm_cvt_bytes = sizeof(AudioConverter);
 
+/* A runtime choice of upsampling method, for front ends that offer one.
+   Negative honours EVV_UPSAMPLE as before; otherwise the value wins, and it
+   is read every time a converter is (re)built rather than once per process,
+   so re-setting the sample rate after calling evv_setUpsampleMethod puts the
+   new method in force at once instead of on the next restart. */
+int evv_upsample_override = -1;
+
+void evv_setUpsampleMethod(int method)
+{
+    evv_upsample_override = method;
+}
+
 /* Read once, because it is an experiment knob and not a setting: an engine
    that changed its mind halfway through an utterance would be comparing two
    things at once. */
@@ -499,6 +511,9 @@ static int cvt_method(void)
     static int decided;
     static int method = CVT_SINC;
 
+    if (evv_upsample_override >= CVT_HOLD && evv_upsample_override <= CVT_SINC)
+        return evv_upsample_override;
+
     if (!decided) {
         const char *say = getenv("EVV_UPSAMPLE");
 
